        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Mutate While Traversing",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for x in &v { v.push(..) }` is **E0502**.",
            "`v.iter_mut()` yields `&mut T` per element safely.",
            "`v.get_mut(i)` returns `Option<&mut T>`, no panic.",
            "`retain`, `drain`, `swap_remove` mutate ==in place==."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two-Phase Borrows"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`v.push(v.len())` compiles, and that surprises everyone.",
            "The `&mut v` is reserved first, still shared-readable.",
            "`v.len()` runs during the reserved phase.",
            "The borrow activates only at the call itself."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Can And Cannot See"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Granularity",
            "Verdict",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`if` branches",
              "sensitive",
              "per-path regions"
            ],
            [
              "struct fields",
              "sensitive",
              "`&mut s.a` + `&s.b` ok"
            ],
            [
              "slice indices",
              "blind",
              "goes via `IndexMut`"
            ],
            [
              "method bodies",
              "opaque",
              "signature only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Signatures Are The Contract",
          "text": "A method returning `&T` borrows the whole receiver even if its body never touches most of it. The checker never reads the body — inline the field access when that bites."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Closures Capture Borrows",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Capture mode is ==inferred== from how the body uses it.",
            "`move` forces ownership into the closure.",
            "A `FnMut` closure holds its `&mut` for its whole life.",
            "Two closures mutating one variable is **E0499**."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Escape Ladder",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Restructure so borrows no longer overlap.",
            "Return owned values, or clone the small ones.",
            "Use indices instead of references.",
            "Reach for `Rc` / `RefCell` for shared graphs.",
            "`unsafe` **last** — you owe the invariants."
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Cloning Is Allowed",
          "text": "Cloning a small value, or on a cold path, is legitimate. Measure before contorting the design."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Checker Limits, Lifetimes as Regions & 'static",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Conservative By Design"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "It rejects some sound programs — false positives happen.",
            "It never accepts an unsound one; that trade is deliberate.",
            "Polonius is the next-generation checker in progress.",
            "Polonius accepts conditional-return-of-borrow that NLL rejects."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "unsafe Does Not Turn It Off",
          "items": [
            "`unsafe` only unlocks raw pointers and a few operations.",
            "Raw pointers are simply ignored by the checker.",
            "Every reference inside `unsafe` is still fully checked."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "How do you actually debug a rejection?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Read the three locations rustc prints, in order.",
            "Borrow starts / conflicting action / later use.",
            "`cargo rustc -- -Z nll-facts` dumps the raw regions.",
            "In practice the three-part message is enough."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Lifetime Is A Region",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Not a duration — a set of program points in the code.",
            "Over that region the reference must stay valid.",
            "Inferred everywhere by default; you rarely write one.",
            "Annotations describe relationships, never extend anything."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Annotations Change Nothing",
          "text": "Writing `'a` does not make data live longer. It only tells the compiler which input a reference points into, so it can check the region you already have."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Dangling Case",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn bad() -> &String {",
            "    let x = String::from(\"gone\");",
            "    &x   // E0106 / E0515: x dies at return",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Outlives And 'static"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`'a: 'b` reads \"'a outlives 'b\" — every point of 'b is in 'a.",
            "`'static` spans the whole program duration.",
            "String literals are `&'static str`; `Box::leak` makes 'static data.",
            "`T: 'static` means \"T holds no non-static references\"."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "'static Is Not Immortal",
          "text": "`T: 'static` is a bound on what `T` may contain, not a promise the value lives forever. An owned `String` satisfies `T: 'static` and still drops at the end of its scope."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Subtyping: a longer lifetime works where a shorter is wanted.",
            "The reverse never holds — that is the whole point."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Interior Mutability Costs",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Checked",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`&mut T`",
              "compile time",
              "free"
            ],
            [
              "`Cell<T>`",
              "by copy",
              "≈ free"
            ],
            [
              "`RefCell<T>`",
              "runtime count",
              "1 check"
            ],
            [
              "`Mutex<T>`",
              "runtime lock",
              "atomic + park"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "RefCell Panics Are Clues",
          "text": "\"already borrowed: BorrowMutError\" is the aliasing bug the compiler would have caught. Treat it as a ==design smell==, not a fix."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Fix Order",
          "text": "**Shorten** the borrow, split the data, copy the small value, restructure ownership — clone last."
        }
      ]
