        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scopes, Drops, Temporaries"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Locals drop in reverse declaration order.",
            "A reference declared before its referent outlives it: E0597.",
            "Temporaries die at the end of the enclosing statement.",
            "Referencing one past that is E0716 temporary dropped while borrowed."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let r = &String::from(\"x\");  // compiles!",
            "// let-bound temporary is extended to r's scope",
            "let n = r.len();"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The Lock Guard Trap",
          "items": [
            "`match m.lock().unwrap() { ... }` holds the guard all block.",
            "Same for an `if let` scrutinee — classic self-deadlock.",
            "Bind the guard with `let g = ...;` and drop it early."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Two Things Named Alike",
          "text": "The lifetime of a reference variable is its scope. The lifetime parameter on a type constrains the referent's region. Lifetimes are erased before codegen: zero representation, zero cost."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Declaring One",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`fn f<'a>(x: &'a str) -> &'a str` — 'a is a generic parameter.",
            "It is declared in the angle brackets like a type parameter.",
            "Without it the compiler cannot tell which input you return.",
            "Anonymous `'_` documents borrowing without naming the region."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {",
            "    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }",
            "}   // output valid for the SHORTER of the two"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Do Not Tie What Is Free",
          "text": "If the output only ever comes from one argument, give the others their own parameter: `fn first<'a,'b>(x: &'a str, _y: &'b str) -> &'a str`. The caller is then free to drop `_y` early."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "fn get(&self) -> &str { self.name }",
            "// desugars to:",
            "fn get<'a>(&'a self) -> &'a str { self.name }"
          ]
        }
      ]
